\h2(listed=false){Contents}

\make_contents

\h2{Introduction}

Currently, there is no way to apply attributes or annotations conditionally.
That is, based on a constant expression,
similar to \tcode{noexcept(\exposid{constant-expression})}.
We propose the following functionality:

\cppblock{
template<class T>
struct S {
  [[if (std::is_floating_point_v<T>) deprecated("f() is only available for integers")]]
  T f();

  // Applying no_unique_address for certain types may be problematic
  [[if (/* ... */) no_unique_address, msvc::no_unique_address]]
  T x;
};
}

Without this capability,
the user always needs to provide distinct declarations
where one has the attribute applied and the other doesn't.

\h2{Motivation}

In recent history, there have been several RFCs in standard library implementations
to provide conditional attributes, such as:
\ul{
  \li{
    \ref("https://discourse.llvm.org/t/extending-clang-lifetimebound-to-take-a-condition/91475"){Extending \tt{[[clang::lifetimebound]]} to take a condition} (LLVM)
  }
  \li{
    \ref("https://discourse.llvm.org/t/rfc-add-a-conditional-no-unique-address-attribute/91530/11"){[RFC] Add a conditional \tt{no_unique_address} attribute} (LLVM)
  }
}

In the latter thread,
several implementers have expressed interest in solving this problem via WG21.
Certain vendor-specific attributes such as \tcode{gnu::no_dangling}
already take a condition.
Furthermore, with C++26 annotations,
the users can essentially create their own attributes,
and those may need to be applied conditionally as well.
Overall, there is clear demand for conditional attributes in a variety of places.

Rather than solving the same problem over and over for each attribute and annotation,
there ought to be a general solution that solves the issue permanently and generally.

\h2{Design}

As shown above, the proposed syntax is:
\cppblock{
// attributes:
[[if (condition) attr0, attr1]] [[attr2]] /* ... */

// annotations:
[[if (condition) =0, =1]] [[=2]] /* ... */
}

The \tcode{if (condition)} part applies to everything
in the following \grammarterm{attribute-list} or \grammarterm{annotation-list}.
To add further attributes or annotations that are not affected by the condition,
one can put these into a separate \grammarterm{attribute-specifier}
(like \tcode{attr2} and \tcode{=2}).

The \tcode{condition} is a converted constant expression of type \tcode{bool},
which is the same approach as for other existing such conditions.

\h3{Alternative syntax considered}

Several alternatives have been considered:
\ul{
  \li{
    \tcode{[[attr0, attr1 requires (condition)]]}.
    The problem with this notation is that it's less clear whether \tcode{requires}
    applies to \tcode{attr1} or to the whole list.
    It looks like an infix operator that applies only to \tcode{attr1}.
    Also, this syntax is more verbose without a clear technical benefit.
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{[[requires (condition) attr0, attr1]]}.
    This is just a more verbose version of the same using \tcode{if}.
  }
}

A key benefit of the \tcode{if} syntax is that there is syntactic precedent.
The user can write
\cppblock{
if (condition) f(), g(), h();
}
\'HORIZONTAL ELLIPSIS' where the \tcode{if} statement
makes all three function calls conditional.
This precedent makes it intuitive that the precedence would work the same in attributes.

\h3{SFINAE-friendliness}

Errors in the condition of the attribute should not be SFINAE-friendly
because this is consistent with the design
of annotations in C++26\iref{dcl.attr.annotation}.
Formally, substituting into the condition is not in the immediate context:

\cppblock{
template<class T>
  [[=T::type()]] void f(T t);
template<class T>
  [[if (T::cond) =0]] void h(T t);

void f(int);
void h(int);

void g() {
  f(0);   // OK, selects f(int)
  f('0'); // error: substituting into the annotation
          // results in an invalid expression T::type

  h(0);   // OK, selects h(int)
  h('0'); // error: substituting into the annotation
          // results in an invalid expression T::cond
}
}

Crucially, the fact that \tcode{char::type} or \tcode{char::cond} is invalid
does not disqualify the respective overloads of \tcode{f} or \tcode{h},
which would have resulted in the \tcode{(int)} overloads being called.

\h3{Interaction with reflection}

If the condition is \tcode{false},
the behavior is as if the attribute or annotation disappeared entirely.
That is, \tcode{std::meta::annotations_of} would yield nothing.

A possible alternative is to add a query \tcode{std::meta::is_enabled},
but this would mean that the user always needs to check whether annotations
are not only present but also enabled.
Existing code which assumes that annotations are always enabled would have its assumption broken.

\h2{Wording}

The changes are relative to \ref("N5054").

